WEBVTT

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.870
Space--the final tourist spot.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.700
There, we'll no doubt find epicurean feats
to stagger the imagination

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.700
and entirely new activities waiting for us!

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.910
In the year 1969, mankind was
about to take a new step.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.790
This is Apollo 11. We are
about to enter lunar orbit.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.040
This is Houston, roger.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.080
Wh-What was that?

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.580
I did it! I'm the first man on the moon!

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:11.660
On that day, July 18th, a lone,
reckless Japanese civilian astronaut

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:17.000
achieved the feat of being the
first man on the moon's surface.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.120
However,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.410
that was not all that happened.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.450
What's the matter? Are you all right?

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.830
Chronic cramps...

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.000
Well, we can't have that.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.200
And so, first contact between mankind
and aliens had been achieved.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.790
Galaxy Century 0001--

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:47.580
To commemorate this first contact
with aliens, it was marked by a new era.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:56.370
"50 years after first contact in 1969,"
"G.C. 0051, is the first year of Reiwa"
♪ Let's go to Isekandar!
Let's go to Isekandar! ♪

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:00.000
"Because of its glory,
the fate of an anime was sealed"
"The Family That Stays Together, Hizakuriger"
♪ Let's go to Isekandar! ♪

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:08.040
The industrial planet Yokkamarché.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.160
Robby, it says it's not known for anything.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.790
Then we don't really
have to stop here, do we?

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.790
You idiot! We have to get
Hizakuriger serviced!

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.790
It took quite a bit of damage
in that last battle!

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:22.540
Hizakuriger and this ship were
both built on Yokkamarché.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:23.830
That's news to me.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:25.540
How do you know it was here?

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.660
Because <i>I </i>was built
on this world, too! I've got

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.450
"Made in Yokkamarché" written right here!

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.830
That's an awful lot of cargo ships.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.370
They bring in the raw materials,
they get processed,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.540
and then the ships carry
away the finished goods.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.370
Looks like Armeni's dock is this way.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:48.790
Who's Armeni?

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.370
The guy who made this ship and Hizakuriger.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.080
It's on the production plate, right?

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.660
--Oh...
--Oh...

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.790
Yokkamarché, I have returned!

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.000
What's the matter?

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.370
It's your first time back home
in a long while. It's okay to cry.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.620
Shut up!

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.870
Even getting all hyped up didn't work.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.250
I have to say, though, nobody's here.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.120
Hey! Is anyone here?!

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.750
Oh, sorry to keep you, sorry to keep you.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.870
This place is completely automated,
so I don't get many people in.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.200
Rouche, how have you been?

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.290
The <i>Nagaya Voyager</i>!
That sure takes me back.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.330
Huh? You aren't Rouche?

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.660
Rouche is my old man.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.200
Oh, so you're Rouche's son. I'm Armeni.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.750
You're the man who built
this spaceship and Hizakuriger?

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.700
That's right. Pretty nice work, right?

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.040
--Hizakuriger got busted up. Can you fix it?

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:52.410
--Huh?
--Hizakuriger got busted up. Can you fix it?

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.660
Huh?! What did you do?!

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:55.500
Fight.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.250
This is just a full-sized figurine
in the shape of Hizakuriger,

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.040
so you shouldn't be fighting with it.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.370
We know that very well.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.250
Aw, what the hell.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:11.750
Oh, this is pretty bad. If I swap some
parts out, though, it can be fixed.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.750
So, do you have anything for me?

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.330
Hmm? No money, if that's what you mean.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.790
I don't mean money!

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.040
Didn't Rouche tell you anything?

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.790
I've been estranged from
my family for a long time, so...

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.040
More than money, what I'd
like to get my hands on

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.870
are old Earth anime cels,
drawings, and recording scripts.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:32.870
<i>Hizakuriger </i>is one of the more popular ones.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.080
You mean that aliens used to watch
Earth-produced anime, too?

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.330
And the old ones, at that?

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.200
Yes, especially the original stuff!

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.410
When they were aired on Earth,
they were too local,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.160
and we didn't even know they existed.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.040
But when they were broadcast
over the galactic networks,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.620
we discovered their appeal.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.290
We then started collecting them.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.750
So that's what my old man would trade?

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:01.160
<i>Hizakuriger </i>in particular was perceived
as an anime that was cursed on Earth.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:02.120
What does he mean?

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:06.080
I don't know, either. It was a show
from a long time before I was born.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.290
It's no exaggeration to say

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:11.040
that any problem you could conceive
of arising in an anime program

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.950
happened to <i>Hizakuriger</i>.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.450
And after the show's original airing,
that bad luck kept coming,

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.080
and the completed series has been lost.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.250
The 12th episode's content is disputed,
and it's regarded as the lost episode.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.700
I have absolutely no idea
what he's talking about.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:26.410
--Same here.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:27.290
--All we have ever seen is the rerun version,
--Same here.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.660
--All we have ever seen is the rerun version,

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.250
so we have been searching
for the elusive 12th episode,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:33.910
but it has yet to be discovered!

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:37.500
Apparently, even the master film
has been destroyed!

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.250
There wasn't any home video
at the time, either.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:42.330
Home video?

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.330
When I went to Earth on my pilgrimage
to the <i>Hizakuriger </i>holy land,

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.910
I happened to get to know Rouche.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:52.620
"Nerima Daikon Radishes"
For some reason, he had some
valuable <i>Hizakuriger </i>materials,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.370
"Nerima Daikon Radishes"
and donated them to my archive.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:57.450
In particular, there were the prized treasures,

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.040
the "bank" cels from the
transforming and combining scene.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.330
In return, I gave him all of my
spending money, and the <i>Nagaya Voyager</i>,

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:07.870
and I even threw in the completed,
actual-size Hizakuriger.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:11.910
So that's how we became rich all of a sudden.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.700
Why did your father have those cels
and whatever else, though?

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.700
I don't know.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:18.830
I've got it!

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:22.290
Back home, they used the <i>Nagaya Voyager</i>
as a sort of storage closet.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.200
I'll go find you something.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.870
All I found was this.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:30.450
Th-This is Tenka-<i>san</i>!

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.750
Valuable cels of the main character!

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:34.790
The hand-traced line indents
are still clearly there!

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:36.250
These are priceless!

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.950
So you'll do the repairs?

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:44.040
I will, I will! Space Ronin Tenka-<i>san</i>
was Director Yarge's first production.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.250
Director Yarge?

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:49.540
Director Yuma Yarge? Of <i>Hizakuriger</i>?

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:53.330
Yuma Yarge is my grandpa's name.

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:57.870
Whoa! You mean, you're
Director Yarge's grandson?

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:02.950
Now it makes sense. That's why
my old man had all of that stuff.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:09.000
So my grandpa was an anime director.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.000
Grandpa passed away when I was five,
so I don't remember a lot about him.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.700
My only vague memory of him
is of him cuddling me.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:19.450
Your father never told you
much about him, huh?

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.120
Ah! Far from that,
he concealed Grandpa's identity

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:27.000
and sold off his keepsakes to get money!
What a damn lousy father!

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.370
Ha, both father and son
turned out to be lowlife scum.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:36.830
The highly automated factories across
the whole surface of Yokkamarché

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.870
have produced enormous wealth,
and thanks to its well-developed healthcare,

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.410
they've gained long lifespans.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.790
Meaning, they have too much time
and money on this world,

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:47.950
and they all have nothing to live
their lives for except their hobbies.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:51.790
Anime Shop "Anime Ersch"...
"De l'allure Anino"...

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:57.540
<i>Samurai Giant: The Movie</i>...
<i>SF Adventurer Gorozinger: The Movie</i>...

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.830
Anime from before I was born, that are
way beyond nostalgia, are super popular.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.290
I don't get any of this.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:05.330
Seriously?

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:07.950
A <i>Hizakuriger </i>specialty shop!

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:11.120
They have figurines,
cups, stationery, T-shirts...

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.950
Ah, they even have <i>Hizakuriger monaka </i>buns!

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:17.700
This is why they aren't known for anything.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.160
Welcome back! How was your sightseeing?

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.000
Ah, those were fantastic! Thank you!

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.620
To repay you, in addition to the repairs,

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:29.250
I'll upgrade the parts for
the Super Hizakuri Beam.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.410
Sure, whatever, just do it quickly.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:35.830
Leave it to me. Just a little
of my famed machine skill, and...

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:39.080
The Hizakuriger repair sequence
is about to begin.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.870
--Wow, how interesting! Can I watch?
--It may be hazardous while
work is in progress,

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:41.870
--Wow, how interesting! Can I watch?
--so please remain behind the yellow line.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:43.870
--Of course.
--so please remain behind the yellow line.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.700
It really is hollow inside!

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:51.120
All right, until repairs are finished,
Robby, would you mind coming with me?

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:52.540
Huh? Where to?

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:57.160
To the <i>Hizakuriger </i>Archive.
Everyone is waiting.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:05.250
Wow, this is amazing!

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:10.750
Yokkamarché seems like a real
industrial planet, all right.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.790
Where the hell is Robby?

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.120
<i>Demon Haircut Afropunch</i>.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.120
<i>Magical Simian Monkey-mimi</i>.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.620
<i>Jankenpon, the Guardian of Space</i>.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.580
<i>Sengoku Majin Gojou-kun</i>.

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:25.200
<i>Machine Yabusame</i>.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.000
--The shops of Yokkamarché will
take all your old Earth anime.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.450
--What is this place?
--The shops of Yokkamarché will
take all your old Earth anime.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:34.000
Anything you have is okay!
Please bring us whatever you have!

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.620
--The Yokkamarchése sure
like their anime, huh?
--We have appraisers on hand! See you there!

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.000
Gras, what do you think? You've watched
a lot of anime, haven't you?

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:44.910
All I know about is the anime
that I watched when I was a child.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.330
I don't know anything about these.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.330
That's Robby's robot!

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.410
So it was originally from an anime.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.410
Ah, there's something written here.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:59.160
A screening is being held today
at the <i>Hizakuriger </i>Archive?

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:00.830
Featuring a special guest?

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.500
I've got it!

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:05.580
Ha! Robby is here.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:08.830
"Galaxy Traveler Hizakuriger"

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:12.040
"Galaxy Traveler Hizakuriger"
<i>Galaxy Traveler Hizakuriger</i>!

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:17.950
♪ With blood boiling hot,
we're off to the galaxy ♪

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:25.450
♪ Even a chance meeting
is decreed by destiny ♪

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:30.830
♪ The way ahead is a far-distant journey ♪

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:36.830
♪ But we can't let
the bad guys go unpunished ♪

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:43.500
♪ Cry out! Combine now, Hizakuriger! ♪

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.500
♪ Gan-Gan! The most stubborn enemies ♪

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.540
♪ Ban-Ban! Exhaust all other options ♪

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.450
♪ Bin-Bin! Tender feelings ♪

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.540
♪ Da! Da! Da-da-da! ♪

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:58.540
♪ Bon-Bon! Don't let your mind wander ♪

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:01.540
♪ Den! Den! Chew on some EM waves! ♪

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.410
--♪ Gin-Gin! Take the galaxy far away ♪

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:04.500
--♪ Gin-Gin! Take the galaxy far away ♪
--Hizakuriger!

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:11.120
--♪ Into the infinite future ♪
--Hizakuriger!

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.330
The reason why old Earth anime is so great

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:22.910
is because it had a passion that
completely ignored any profit margins.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:28.160
Ahem. <i>Galaxy Traveler Hizakuriger</i>!
"These travels are dangerous, too!"

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.000
"We depart knowing full well the danger!"

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:36.580
--I invited a special guest today.
--Huh? What? Did we already start?

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:41.870
This is Director Yuma Yarge's
grandson, Robby Yarge.

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:46.080
Thank you. But my grandfather
passed away when I was five,

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.120
so I don't remember much about him.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.540
He had such poor luck
when it came to his productions,

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.410
so I'm sure he had a hard time of it.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:55.200
I'll bet you can still tell us some
cherished stories, though, right?

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.120
Sure, ask me anything.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.250
Robby, what's your favorite episode?

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:00.950
Um... which one's yours?

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:02.870
Mine is Episode 3 from the early series.

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:04.200
The early series was respectable.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:05.500
Mid-series would be Episode 7.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:08.250
The mid-series was muddled,
and the late series was a breakthrough.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:10.370
<i>Hizakuriger </i>was only 13 episodes,

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:13.870
but the beginning, middle, and end all
had a different color to the production.

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:15.750
You say the early series was respectable,

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:18.370
but Episode 1 and Episode 2's openings
weren't completed in time,

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.160
so that version used cuts from the main story

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:21.910
slapped together by another director, right?

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:25.330
The corrected versions also had more
and more dummy cuts swapped in,

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:27.120
and it wasn't finished until the last episode.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:28.370
That's right!

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:31.950
Collecting those was a real chore.

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:36.040
The reruns had the completed opening
switched in for each episode.

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:39.450
We didn't even know how
it was originally broadcast.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:42.120
--Our generation only knows about
it through reruns, after all!

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.910
--H-Huh?
--Our generation only knows about
it through reruns, after all!

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:48.790
Speaking of not making the cut,
the entire schedule didn't make it, either;

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.910
it was supposed to be two cours,
26 episodes, but there are only 13.

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.950
Whenever they dropped some,
they had to show reruns,

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:57.160
so at the end, the recaps and the reruns
would get run back-to-back!

00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:59.580
Well, it was originally
52 episodes over four cours,

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:01.000
but it got cut down to two cours.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:04.410
Earthlings made first contact
during its original run,

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:08.410
and having Earthlings learn of
actual aliens really hurt the show.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.870
Sure, the whole affair seems
ridiculous from where we're sitting,

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:15.950
but in fact, that's the reason for the
cult following the show has gained.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.080
The quality of the toy line was atrocious, too.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:20.910
At the time, Earth toy
technology was really poor,

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:25.370
and they couldn't reproduce the complex
action of a transforming robot.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:27.000
Plus the proportions are all screwed up.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.750
But it's the terrible quality
that gives them their style.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:32.950
Though apparently,
they didn't sell at all on Earth.

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:36.790
Later on, the studio started
doing as they pleased,

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:39.000
and they had a string of incredible episodes!

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.290
But Earthlings didn't realize
how good they were.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:45.250
The studio was on the knife edge
of failing, after all.

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:47.580
The last episode had intrigue
running all throughout it,

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.500
and without knowing
anything about what happens,

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:54.450
the Super Hizakuri Beam is installed,
but ends up never being used.

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:56.200
--Oh, yeah! The Hizakuri--
--However, for us, who have
only seen the rebroadcasts,

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:58.080
--However, for us, who have
only seen the rebroadcasts,

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.000
what we <i>really </i>yearn for is the
yet-unseen legendary Episode 12!

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.660
I want it! I want to see it,
but it doesn't exist!

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.790
With storyboards and
screenplay by Director Yuma,

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.830
and animated solely by character
designer and producer Kinomata!

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:11.750
It was supposed to be the highest quality,

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:14.580
but after it aired, it became a lost episode.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:17.290
A lost episode that was
removed from rebroadcasts,

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:20.580
and even the master film
was disposed of! Unbelievable!

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:24.830
We tried to capture the signal
directly from the original broadcast,

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:27.790
but the attenuation and noise
made it impossible to reproduce!

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:30.040
We looked and looked, regardless of the cost,

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:33.330
but we have yet to locate
any film or video data from it!

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:36.620
Robby, did you hear anything
from your grandfather

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:38.080
about the legendary Episode 12?

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:39.410
Uh...

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:42.910
After Episode 12 finished airing,
its content became an issue,

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:44.660
and it was erased from existence.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:47.580
Some surmise that some kind of
pressure was brought to bear.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:49.200
--That's--
--The film, the script,

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:52.080
and all other materials were disposed of.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.950
Word is that Director Yuma left
the anime industry heartbroken.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:58.120
The only material belonging
to Episode 12 that remains

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:02.370
is the comment written for the preview
of the recording script for Episode 11.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.950
"A terrible plot is hatched by the Saturnians!

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.500
Hizakuriger is faced with its greatest crisis!

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:10.580
Next time, 'The Saturnian
Gakudojin.' See you then!"

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:15.410
And what can be inferred from the
episodes before, after, and incident to it.

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:19.830
In Episode 13, there's the line
"The Hizakuri Beam failed to work earlier,"

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:22.660
but there were no scenes that
came before that showed that.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:25.450
Which means that the enemy the
Hizakuri Beam didn't work against

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:28.120
is thought to have appeared in Episode 12.

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:29.870
However, being the final episode,

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:32.200
consistency with the
previous episode was ignored.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.410
The current speculation for Episode 12's story

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:38.370
is that the Saturnians were developing
a new kind of nuclear weapon,

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:41.540
and Hizakuriger went off to put a stop to it,

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.250
but they had a powerful shield equipped,
and it couldn't get through.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.660
But, they still narrowly put
a stop to the Saturnians' plot.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:50.370
Or so everyone says,
but what do you think, Robby?

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:53.000
Hmm, I think that's completely wrong.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.290
Huh?!

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.500
H-H-How can you say such a thing?!

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:58.200
Because I've seen it.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:01.250
Huh? But you weren't alive when
it was originally aired, right?

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:04.330
As I was listening to all of you,
I remembered something.

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:06.750
That must have been <i>Hizakuriger</i>.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:10.500
It opens with the robot's chest glowing,
then goes into a flashback,

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.500
and the whole episode ends up
taking place in their youths,

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:15.830
so I didn't even realize what I was watching.

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:17.700
There wasn't any opening
attached to it, either.

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:19.370
Wh-Where did you see it?!

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:21.160
Whenever I went over to visit
my grandpa as a kid,

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.330
he would always show me these movies.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:25.790
He threaded the film into
the projector like this.

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:27.040
The master film!

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:30.160
That's it! Director Yarge had it!

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:32.120
Thanks to you, I had a fond memory of--

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:34.250
So then?! Where is that film now?!

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:37.000
--Where?! Where?!
--Where is it?! Where is it?!

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.950
It got put into Grandpa's casket.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:39.660
Huh?!

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:41.750
If it's anywhere now, it's up in Heaven.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.910
But you saw Episode 12 several times, right?

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:50.750
I saw it, sure, but I wasn't
even five at the time.

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:53.370
I hardly remember any of it.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:58.700
Latent memories from childhood
are a wondrous thing, Robby.

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:05.040
I'm going to help you re-mem-ber.

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:17.040
Huh? Huh?

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.080
Hold on, what kind of "play" is this?

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:26.750
Earthlings are unaware that they
have been given a magnificent gift.

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:29.950
A gift we want so bad we can taste it.

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:31.500
What are you talking about?!

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:33.870
The device you're currently sitting in

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:37.750
is able to directly access a person's
brain and extract its memories.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.830
There are many lost videos of
old Earth anime that have been lost,

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.250
and not just <i>Hizakuriger</i>.

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:47.750
We use this to collect those lost videos.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:49.830
So, um, does it hurt?

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:53.910
The pain is not that bad, but it
does leave you with memory loss.

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:55.450
What kind of memory loss?

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:59.540
You forget who you are.
You forget <i>what </i>you are.

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:01.500
You forget about Earth, too.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.000
Hey, you mean...?

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.410
--Robby, you are a valuable individual.
--Stop! Don't! Don't do it! Don't!

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:10.290
--I have great expectations.
--Don't do this! Stop!!

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.500
The time when he was five years old

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:22.660
--would be 26 years ago in Earth time!
--Somebody help!

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:29.000
Hatchi! Ikku!

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:32.000
--It's Mr. Yuma!
--Robby? Robby?

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.330
--That's Director Yuma!
--Robby? Robby?

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:40.620
Robby, want to watch anime
again with Grandpa?

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:42.160
--Mm-hmm.
--It's starting! It's starting!

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:43.620
--The elusive 12th episode of <i>Hizakuriger</i>!
--It's starting! It's starting!

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.290
--The elusive 12th episode of <i>Hizakuriger</i>!
--Make sure this is recording!

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:47.660
Stop! Don't touch me!

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:51.370
This is one of Grandpa's keepsakes.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:58.290
Precious stuff!

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:02.080
It's my memory!

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.160
What do you want?!

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.120
Why, you! What the hell
are you doing to my Robby?!

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:10.500
--Stay out of this!
--Stay out of this!

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:17.950
I-I'm anti-violence!

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.410
Robby, are you all right?

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:29.080
Mr. Yang...?

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:31.450
You saved me! You saved me, Mr. Yang!

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:35.040
Heh, I'll add it to your tab, Robby.

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:41.450
Stop interfering!

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:43.160
Why, you...

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.540
Stop that!

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:53.370
Never underestimate the
Yokkamarchése desire to collect!

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.750
Never...

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:58.370
...sell short...

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:00.250
...an Earthling's lust!

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:10.660
Stop! Don't do this!

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:13.040
Stop this!

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:14.450
Silence!

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:17.000
I-Is that...

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:21.660
...a T-shirt from my favorite anime,
<i>Binan Shonen Love-Love</i>?

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:22.910
Why do you have this?

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:25.830
This is a show that
<i>Hizakuriger</i>'s animation director,

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:28.330
Mr. Kinomata, did the character designs for.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:29.700
Do you have any video?!

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:32.580
--Huh?
--I said, do you have any video?!

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:37.750
Oh, Robby is back.

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.250
Robby, the repairs are done.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:44.080
We're getting out of here! Hurry!

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:45.080
What happened?

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:49.040
I'll explain later! Yokkamarché
is a hell of a bad world!

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:57.250
Whew. I never imagined
I'd ever be rescued by Yang.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:01.000
Ikku, why didn't you tell us
this planet was so shady?!

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:04.750
Oh, apparently, I got reset
when I was on Earth.

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:08.660
I don't have any data left from
before you turned me on. Sorry.

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:16.290
--Stop! Stop!
--<i>Binan Shonen Love-Love</i>

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:19.080
was an anime from when
I was in grade school.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:22.620
The PTA took issue with its content,
and it was canceled.

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:25.040
--Is it that bad?
--Is it that sexy?

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:27.620
It will make you go "<i>kyun</i>," you dumbasses!

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:30.790
I'm about to show you its first
outstanding episode, Episode 4.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:32.790
Let's see, is this it?

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:48.120
Grandpa's anime, huh?

00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:00.330
Hey, Yuma, what do you want
to be when you grow up?

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:03.790
I want to make manga movies.
What about you, Rock?

00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:05.500
I'm going to be an astronaut.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:09.370
Outer space, huh? A manga movie about
outer-space adventures would be neat.

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:11.620
Okay, then when both
of our dreams come true,

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:14.290
let's blast off into space together.

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:16.330
On a journey of adventure!

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.950
"To be continued"

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:53.000
"Next time"
"#10 'Never Get Involved in a Buddies' Quarrel'"

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:56.370
"Next time"
"#10 'Never Get Involved in a Buddies' Quarrel'"
Next time, "Never Get Involved
in a Buddies' Quarrel."

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:56.950
"Next time"
"#10 'Never Get Involved in a Buddies' Quarrel'"

00:23:57.200 --> 00:24:01.910
"A huge fight erupts between Robby and Hatchi"
"What will become of the trip to Isekandar?"

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:06.950
"Doesn't really matter
what becomes of it, right?"
"It says 'never get involved,' right? (heh)"
